= What Was in That Old Diagram

The opening lines of #series-link("20220608-mmo-server-from-scratch(0)-introduction", "(0)") still hold up: the material I could find either talked about architecture in vague terms or discussed some algorithm purely on paper. I was a complete outsider, abstract material was hard to turn into practice, so I decided to build a server myself. I chose `Elixir` because it supports high concurrency natively, and the ideas of supervision trees and failing early won me over.

The overall diagram was roughly: `auth_server` handles login, `gate_server` accepts client TCP connections, `agent_server` runs non-scene logic (marked as undecided at the time), `scene_server` runs scenes, `world_server` coordinates scene allocation, the data layer was planned on `Mnesia`, and `beacon_server` served as the cluster's fixed rendezvous point. Client connections followed `one process per client`. I wrote at the time that much of this was guesswork, and real performance would only be known once it was built.

For roughly the next half year, the series kept building along that diagram.

#series-link("20220610-mmo-server-from-scratch(1)-beacon-server", "(1)") built the beacon first. Forming an Elixir cluster needs a fixed node that can be reached at startup; by default the cluster is fully connected, with every node linked to every other. Nodes register their "resources" and "requirements" with the beacon, and the beacon introduces registered nodes according to those requirements.

#series-link("20221016-mmo-server-from-scratch(2)-gate-server", "(2)") built the gateway. Clients connect to `gate_server` over a TCP socket, messages use Protobuf, each connection gets one `TcpConnection` process, and after registering through the beacon, traffic is forwarded to the business servers. The supervision tree is `Interface`, `TcpAcceptor`, and a set of `TcpConnection` processes.

#series-link("20221016-mmo-server-from-scratch(3)-scene-server-intro", "(3)") introduced the scene server, whose first phase did only two things: movement sync, and players entering or leaving the game. AOI used a cross linked list. Players moving across scene servers, or entering and leaving the open world through dungeons or teleports, I put off until "after the code matures later". This post was also where I settled the division of labor: heavy computation goes to Rust NIFs, while concurrent messaging and broadcasting stay in Elixir.

Next, #series-link("20221021-mmo-server-from-scratch(4)-scene-server-aoi-algorithm", "(4)") and the post on the AOI supervision tree put the cross linked list into a Rust data structure and hooked it into an Elixir supervision tree. Then came time synchronization, and from #series-link("20221026-mmo-server-from-scratch(6)-scene-server-movement-sync-0", "(6)") to #series-link("20221124-mmo-server-from-scratch(10)-scene-server-movement-sync-4-player-movement-client", "(10)") the first round of movement sync: the command queue, server-side coordinate calculation, entering the scene, and then the server and client sides of player movement. In those posts the client is a subscriber, and the server sends it the people around it.

So the 2022 setup already had the server computing coordinates: (6) had the command queue and server-side coordinate calculation, and in (3) heavy computation went through NIFs. Four things changed later: the contract got tighter, the transport changed, ownership of the world truth moved, and cross-Scene finally made it onto the agenda.

#series-link("20221125-mmo-server-from-scratch(11)-scene-server-player-process-reorg-0", "(11)") through (14) reorganized the player processes, with a dedicated post on #series-link("20221127-mmo-server-from-scratch(13)-rustler-introduction", "Rustler") in between. (15) and (16) thought about a physics engine (movement validation, hit detection) and movement error, which called for some way to visualize things. So (17) through (23) built the Visualize Server with Phoenix and PIXI to view the character list, coordinates, and facing; it was a debugging and observation tool.

Finally, #series-link("20221216-mmo-server-from-scratch(24)-scene-server-rapier-introduction", "(24)") through (28) settled on Rapier 3D, using the Kinematic Character Controller to drive character movement, with positions represented by colliders, and the Elixir side holding a `PhySys` through `PhysicsManager`. The series stopped there, leaving walking and jump sync over complex terrain for the next phase.

= Which Guesses Were Later Replaced

Opening this repository again four years later, the biggest changes are in several of the foundations on that old diagram. The table below sets the ideas from the old posts against the current state of the public repository.

