TCP was replaced by QUIC because TCP has head-of-line blocking and high latency. Game messages have different priorities depending on how time-sensitive they are, and movement sync is the most typical kind; TCP handles these poorly. QUIC separates the reliable control streams from time-sensitive datagrams: per RFC 9000, data within a stream is ordered, there is no global arrival order across streams, and a DATAGRAM is sent and received whole at the application layer. The fact that "the two streams go their own way" comes up again in the handoff section.

The database went from Mnesia back to PostgreSQL for a plain reason: Mnesia is too limited, it is hard to write code against, and its related libraries are inactive with few users. I chose to go back to the mainstream and use PostgreSQL, which everyone uses; persistence today is PostgreSQL plus Ecto.

NavMesh and the voxel truth are two separate topics. The `NavMesh` in (28) belongs to movement execution: how a character walks over terrain and how it jumps. The voxel truth answers where world state lives: the voxel world is the single source of truth, collision reads authoritative occupancy, and the world the client sees all comes from it.

The current spatial contract is Y-up, in meters.

= Who Owns What Now

Drawing today's responsibilities as a diagram shows four boundaries: who manages the connection, who manages this person's movement, who is allowed to change the world, and who hands a person from one authority domain to another.

#arch-fig("Current responsibilities: connection, player process, the only writable world")[
```pintora
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant G as Gate
  participant S as Session
  participant P as Player
  participant Sc as Scene
  participant W as World
  C->>G: QUIC streams and DATAGRAMs
  G->>S: Entry registration and identity
  G->>P: Input goes straight to that player
  P->>Sc: Immutable movement results
  Sc->>W: Read-only collision / world intents
  W-->>Sc: Replica and authoritative deltas
```
]

The connection side consists of the Gate, authentication, and `WorldServer.Session`. Clients come in over QUIC, and the Gate handles authentication, decoding, and per-channel routing. `WorldServer.Session` is the single registry for character sessions: it manages identity and session generations, handles duplicate logins, and commits the switch during a handoff.

On the scene side, the Scene owns common time, membership, and publishing collision versions. Each authenticated identity has one `Player` process, which exclusively owns the input slot, movement state, simulation progress, and acknowledged prefix. After entry binding, the Gate hands input directly to this owner. The replication layer consumes the immutable results produced by Player and distributes them to observers. AOI now uses a grid algorithm with hysteresis.

On the world side, only `VoxelRegion.World` can write. The truth is a generated or baked base, plus every committed edit after it. The `Replica` on a Scene node is a read-only regional view, and collision derivatives are rebuilt from the authoritative world or a local read-only copy. At startup the authority node bakes the base first and then starts World; only once the base is complete does the application finish starting, and only then does the Gate that depends on it start listening.

Orchestration belongs to `WorldServer`: routing, controlled handoff, and an explicit JSON topology. The server is built and run on Linux, the runtime is Elixir and OTP, native computation goes through Rustler, and the byte contracts are gathered in the pure library `mmo_contracts`, divided into Session, Movement, and Voxel.

Server authority is still the global iron rule, and it covers more than in 2022: movement, AOI, voxels, and object state all defer to the server, while the client handles preview, presentation, and sending intents. The confirmed voxel state comes only from authoritative results; when a source is incomplete it is explicitly rejected, which keeps the client's world consistent with the authority at all times.

= Movement Sync: A Stricter Contract, a New Transport

The server-side computation and broadcasting to nearby players from (6) to (10), and the Rapier hooked in through NIFs in (24) to (28), all carry on in today's repository: the shared Rapier movement kernel is still there, and the server still advances movement. What changed is the contract around it.

Collision reads authoritative voxel occupancy, and whether you can walk somewhere is decided by the server's world. The protocol went from Protobuf messages scattered across the gateway back then to the pure library `mmo_contracts`, and the old movement protocol was retired along the way. The timeline uses a fixed step and a public tick, and I like this line a lot: physics time is never made by counting packets.

The transport became QUIC, for the reasons given in the previous section. The server-authoritative movement loop has passed acceptance, covering bootstrap, Ready, movement, and real two-client joint testing.

On the client side, I will only cover concepts. It is server-authoritative state sync, plus prediction and correction for your own character. Logical correction is layered separately from Mesh and camera smoothing; remote players are interpolated from timestamped snapshots; network statistics are produced by callbacks as immutable snapshots, and the game thread only consumes them. The server's contract of "input goes into the authority, confirmation comes back" maps to three layers on the client: prediction, correction, and presentation.

For concurrency, the Scene advances players separately: one `Player` process per player, with replication then split across multiple observer workers.

= Cross-Scene Handoff: The Thing Postponed Back Then

The cross-scene movement postponed in (3) is now required. The world is cut into multiple Scene authority domains, with partitions as configured static ranges; when a player leaves the authority box of their Scene, the writer has to change. A character has only one writer at any moment, each input is executed exactly once, and state in a new generation only accepts inputs and snapshots from the new generation.

This round of controlled handoff passed acceptance on two independent BEAM virtual machines on the same host, with both sides using an explicitly aligned common tick zero point. Commit `981f27fe` brought the local Replica and controlled handoff into the public repository.

The flow is roughly as follows.

#arch-fig("Controlled handoff sequence (conceptual)")[
```pintora
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant P as Source Player
  participant G as Gate
  participant S as Session
  participant D as Target Scene
  P->>G: Leaves this region's authority range
  G->>P: Seal from the same sender as earlier inputs
  G->>S: Reserve a new session generation
  S->>G: Prepared
  G->>P: Notify switch
  @note over P,D: Integration starts only after target Ready
  P->>G: Ready
  G->>S: Commit handoff
  S->>D: Source releases first, then target activates
```
]

Each of the two nodes runs a Scene and a local read-only Replica, and the authoritative world is written in only one place. Neighboring regions exchange read-only replication frames so that people on either side of the boundary can see each other; these records are for observation only, and collision decisions are still made by the local Player.

After crossing the boundary, the player stops first. The Gate that is forwarding the input initiates the seal, relying on OTP's "messages from the same sender are ordered" semantics to guarantee that the seal lands after all earlier inputs. `WorldServer.Session` reserves a new session generation, the connection notifies the client to switch, and the commit happens only after a Ready matching the new identity and cut point arrives. The source releases the writer first, then the target activates. Native physics handles stay on their own nodes, and the target side rebuilds its own.

The target side rebuilds history from the common baseline it already has locally, plus the collision checkpoint brought by the source, then imports the input slot; integration starts only after Ready. Input sequence numbers and ticks stay continuous across the handoff.

From the client's point of view, before and after the switch it is the same character, the same input sequence, and the same prediction history; the person crosses the boundary carrying the state they have already reached.

Voxel edits are executed by a serial worker started lazily on the connection, while the Gate session keeps handling input and Ready. Control goes over one reliable stream and edits over another, and there is no global arrival order between the two streams, so the handoff has to tolerate late edits. The current scope is two Scenes sharing the same world. Later, tests were added to the repository to verify that effect receipts that have to cross the cut point still arrive after a real scene handoff.

= What Stayed, What Was Replaced, and What Comes Next

What stayed: Elixir and OTP process and supervision boundaries; the division of labor set in (3), with heavy computation in Rust NIFs; Rapier as the character movement kernel, now part of a kernel shared by both ends; one process per player, which now exclusively owns input, state, and the acknowledged prefix; server-computed movement since (6), with a stricter contract; AOI is still there, switched to a grid algorithm; and the repository is still the same public repository.

What was replaced: the official entry point went from TCP plus Protobuf to QUIC; the cluster went from a beacon plus full connectivity to an explicit JSON topology; the data layer went from Mnesia to PostgreSQL; the voxel truth moved from in-Scene chunk processes to `VoxelRegion.World`; the Visualize Server and the old Interface modules left along with the old pipeline; and cross-Scene handoff went from "deal with it later" to an accepted capability.

What is done is the server-authoritative movement loop, and controlled handoff between two Scenes on the same host (local Replica). Next up are full capacity acceptance, cross-machine deployment and clocks, and failover and dynamic partitioning.

At the end of (0) I wrote: since I couldn't find anyone like-minded, I was fighting alone, and I didn't know how long I could keep going. Four years on, the repository is still here, the mainline is still moving forward, and several of the foundations on that old diagram have been replaced by my own hand. Guesses are there to be tested; the next post will keep going from here.
